line 581)\n  │   Purpose: Revert root layer to recover first layer's two children (line 581)\n  │\n  └─ [All tree operations complete; tree maintained in-place in single buffer]\n```\n\n**Kernel Launch Details (Segment Tree)**:\n- **`bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`** (line 1339):\n  - Threads: 256/block, Grid: `(domain_size / (256 * 8))`\n  - Shared mem: 64 KB/block\n  - Fuses: bitrev + tree layers 0+1 in one kernel\n  \n- **`frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`** (line 28):\n  - Threads: 256/block (default from `kernel_launch_params`)\n  - Grid: `⌈half / 256⌉` blocks\n  - Per-layer operation: parallelizes over half-sized output elements\n  \n- **`frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`** (line 856):\n  - Threads: 256/block (reduced from 1024 for register headroom, line 918)\n  - Grid: `⌈half_i1 / 256⌉` blocks\n  - Fuses two consecutive layers\n\n---\n\n#### **Call Chain: GKR Rounds Phase**\n\n**Main Loop**: `for round in 1..total_rounds` (line 655)\n\nFor each GKR round `j = round + 1`:\n\n```\nGKR Round j (code round):\n  ├─ Setup eq_buffer: SqrtEqLayers::from_xi(&xi_prev[1..]) (line 663)\n  │\n  ├─ First sumcheck round (round 0): ALWAYS via do_sumcheck_round_and_revert()\n  │   ├─ frac_compute_round_and_revert() ──→ CUDA: _frac_compute_round_and_revert (line 144)\n  │   │   Calls: compute_round_and_revert_kernel (line 1037) + final_reduce_block_sums\n  │   │   Fuses: tree layer revert + sumcheck compute round 0\n  │   │\n  │   └─ eq_buffer.drop_layer() (line 381) — no GPU kernel, CPU side\n  │\n  ├─ Remaining sumcheck rounds (rounds 1 to round-1): Choose strategy (line 680)\n  │\n  └─ Strategy 1: FoldEval (line 718–819)\n     ├─ For each remaining xi_j:\n     │   ├─ BufferScheduler decides target (ping-pong vs in-place, lines 720–787)\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ do_fused_sumcheck_round(src_buffer, dst_buffer) ──→ CUDA: _frac_compute_round_and_fold\n     │   │   Calls: compute_round_and_fold_kernel (line 235) + final_reduce_block_sums\n     │   │   Fuses: fold by r_prev + compute s'(1), s'(2)\n     │   │   **Out-of-place** (ping-pong between layer & work_buffer)\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ do_fused_sumcheck_round_inplace(buffer) ──→ CUDA: _frac_compute_round_and_fold_inplace\n     │   │   Calls: compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel (line 323) + final_reduce_block_sums\n     │   │   **In-place** (reads 2*pq_size, writes pq_size to same buffer)\n     │   │\n     │   └─ eq_buffer.drop_layer() (line 431, 477)\n     │\n     └─ Final fold: fold_ef_frac_columns() ──→ CUDA: _frac_fold_fpext_columns (line 155)\n        Calls: fold_ef_columns_kernel (line 204)\n        Standalone kernel, no compute (final inner round)\n\n  └─ Strategy 2: PrecomputeM (line 821–1054)\n     ├─ Loop: while base < stop (line 853)\n     │   ├─ Choose window width w (line 856)\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ Build M-matrix: frac_precompute_m_build_raw() ──→ CUDA: _frac_precompute_m_build (line 193)\n     │   │   Calls: precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold, W> (line 515)\n     │   │           + precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel (line 1211)\n     │   │   Instances: ONE per tail_tile (num_blocks computed at line 893)\n     │   │   Purpose: Compute M matrix with tail-parallelism\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ For each round t in window (w rounds):\n     │   │   ├─ frac_precompute_m_eval_round_raw() ──→ CUDA: _frac_precompute_m_eval_round (line 209)\n     │   │   │   Calls: precompute_m_eval_round_kernel (line 1233)\n     │   │   │   Grid: 1 block (small kernel operating on 2^{2w} M-matrix)\n     │   │   │   Purpose: Extract s'_t(1), s'_t(2) from M\n     │   │   │\n     │   │   ├─ observe_and_update() (CPU, line 955)\n     │   │   │   Reconstructs s evaluations from GPU output\n     │   │   │   Samples challenge r\n     │   │   │\n     │   │   └─ eq_buffer.drop_layer() (line 954)\n     │   │\n     │   └─ Multifold: frac_multifold_raw() ──→ CUDA: _frac_multifold (line 218)\n     │       Calls: multifold_kernel<W> (line 783, templated on W)\n     │       Grid: `⌈tail_size / 256⌉`\n     │       Purpose: Fold w (or w+1) variables at once\n     │\n     ├─ Tail rounds (if any): Fallback to fold-eval path (lines 1002–1045)\n     │\n     └─ Final fold: fold_ef_frac_columns_inplace() (line 1049)\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. TREE STRUCTURE: LEVELS, PER-TRACE VS PER-LEVEL OPERATIONS**\n\n#### **Segment Tree Layout**\n- **Total Rounds**: `total_rounds = log2(total_leaves)` = n\n- **Layers**: n+1 layers (layer 0 = root, layer n = leaves)\n- **Memory**: Single buffer of size `2^n` (input leaves), reused in-place\n- **Ordering**: Bit-reversed for coalesced memory access\n\n#### **Per-Trace Operations (INDEPENDENT, PARALLELIZABLE)**\nEach tree layer build is a per-trace operation:\n- **Thread i processes**: pairs `(layer[i], layer[i + half])`\n- **No data dependencies between threads** within a layer\n- **All threads can run in parallel** (different indices → different memory addresses)\n- **Synchronization**: Only implicit via kernel barrier at end\n\n**Examples**:\n1. `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`: Each thread `idx` computes `layer[idx] = frac_add(layer[idx], layer[idx + half])` independently\n2. `multifold_kernel`: Each thread processes a unique tail index independently\n3. `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`: 2D block grid where each thread (u,v) accumulates M[u,v] over independent tail tiles\n\n#### **Per-Level Operations (SEQUENTIAL)**\n- **Levels must be processed sequentially**: output of level i becomes input to level i+1\n- **Level i must complete** before level i+1 starts (implicit via kernel launch sequencing)\n- **No loop over tree levels in GPU code**: tree building is fused into launchers\n\n#### **Synchronization Points**\n1. **After each kernel launch**: implicit host-side sync via kernel completion\n2. **eq_buffer.drop_layer()**: CPU-side operation (no GPU sync needed)\n3. **Tree reverts**: Sequential per round (one layer per GKR round)\n4. **Block reductions**: Implicit via `static_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel\n\n---\n\n### **3. KEY CUDA KERNELS: STRUCTURE & BEHAVIOR**\n\n#### **A. Segment Tree Kernels**\n\n| Kernel | File | Line | Purpose | Fused With |\n|--------|------|------|---------|-----------|\n| `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel` | gkr.cu | 1339 | Bitrev + tree layers 0+1 | Bitrev + 2-layer tree |\n| `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel` | gkr.cu | 28 | Single layer tree build/revert | Nothing |\n| `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel` | gkr.cu | 856 | Two consecutive tree layers | 2-layer build |\n\n**Thread Model**:\n- Each thread idx: `layer[idx] = op(layer[idx], layer[idx + half])`\n- **Independence**: Each thread's read/write addresses are unique → no conflicts\n- **Parallelism**: Linear in `half` (output size)\n\n---\n\n#### **B. Sumcheck Compute Kernels**\n\n| Kernel | Fused Ops | In-Place? | Outputs |\n|--------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n| `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` | Revert + Compute | YES | s'(1), s'(2) |\n| `compute_round_and_fold_kernel` | Compute + Fold | NO | s'(1), s'(2) + folded pq |\n| `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel` | Compute + Fold | YES | s'(1), s'(2) + folded pq |\n| `compute_round_block_sum_kernel` | Compute only | N/A | s'(1), s'(2) |\n\n**All 4 kernels**:\n- **Per-trace parallelism**: YES (each thread processes independent pairs)\n- **Thread idx**: processes pair `idx` over all 2^n hypercube points\n- **Local accumulators**: local0 (for s'(1)), local1 (for s'(2))\n- **Block reduction**: Sum-reduce within block, write to `tmp_block_sums[block * 2 + i]`\n- **Final reduction**: Separate kernel reduces block sums → final output (2 values)\n\n**Critical fusion**: Fold + Compute are fused to reduce kernel launches and GMem traffic:\n- Fuses 2 kernels (fold + compute) → 1 kernel + 1 reduction kernel\n- Each trace: folds 4 pairs of pq values simultaneously with compute\n\n---\n\n#### **C. Precompute-M Kernels**\n\n| Kernel | Line | Purpose | Parallelism |\n|--------|------|---------|-------------|\n| `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold, W>` | 515 | Build M-matrix partial blocks | Over tail points |\n| `precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel` | 1211 | Reduce partial M blocks | Over M entries |\n| `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel` | 1233 | Extract s'(t) from M | 1 block only |\n| `multifold_kernel<W>` | 783 | Fold w variables at once | Over tail indices |\n\n**M-build (line 515)**:\n- **Grid**: `num_blocks = ⌈2^{tail_n} / tail_tile⌉` (1 block per tail_tile points)\n- **Block**: `(2^w, 2^w)` threads = m² threads, one per M entry (u,v)\n- **Operation per thread**: Accumulate contribution `M[u,v] += sum over tail_tile`\n- **Per-tail-block independence**: Each block processes disjoint tail points → parallelizable\n- **Shared memory**: `(4 * (2^w + 1) * tail_batch + tail_batch) * sizeof(FpExt)` with bank-conflict padding\n\n**M-reduce (line 1211)**:\n- **Grid**: `⌈2^{2w} / 128⌉` blocks\n- **Operation**: Sum over all `num_blocks` partial contributions for each M entry\n- **Block reduction**: Each block sums 128 entries in parallel\n\n**M-eval (line 1233)**:\n- **Grid**: 1 block, 256 threads\n- **Operation**: Contract M with eq_r_prefix and eq_suffix to compute s'(1), s'(2)\n- **Small kernel**: operates on 2^{2w} M-matrix (w=3 → 64x64 = 4096 entries)\n\n**Multifold (line 783)**:\n- **Grid**: `⌈tail_size / 256⌉` blocks\n- **Block**: 256 threads\n- **Thread out_idx**: computes output `[out_idx, tail_size + out_idx]` (two poly outputs)\n- **Operation**: For each of 2^w coefficients, sum-accumulate weighted by eq_r_window\n\n---\n\n### **4. INSTANCE COUNTS FOR KEY KERNELS**\n\n#### **Segment Tree Build**\n- **`bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`**: 1 instance (if domain_size > 1024)\n- **`frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`**: 1 instance (first layer after bitrev, or fallback)\n- **`frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`**: `⌈(total_rounds - start_layer_i) / 2⌉` instances\n  - Typical: 10 instances for 20-round computation\n\n#### **GKR Rounds (Total Sumcheck Rounds)**\n- **Total sumcheck rounds across all GKR rounds**: `∑(round) = 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (total_rounds-1) = O(total_rounds²)`\n  - For total_rounds=20: 0+1+2+...+19 = 190 sumcheck rounds\n\n#### **Per-GKR-Round Kernel Instances** (assuming fold-eval strategy)\nFor GKR round j (code: round):\n- **`compute_round_and_revert_kernel`**: 1 instance (first sumcheck round 0)\n- **`compute_round_and_fold_kernel`** or **`compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel`**: `round - 1` instances (sumcheck rounds 1 to round-1)\n- **`fold_ef_columns_kernel`**: 1 instance (final fold after last sumcheck round)\n- **`static_final_reduce_block_sums`**: 1 instance per compute kernel (reduces block sums)\n\n**Total for all GKR rounds** (fold-eval path, total_rounds=20):\n- `compute_round_and_revert_kernel`: 19 instances (one per GKR round 1..19)\n- Fused compute+fold kernels: 0+1+2+...+18 = 171 instances\n- Fold-only kernels: 19 instances\n- **~210 kernel launches total** (dominated by fused compute+fold)\n\n#### **Precompute-M Path** (if enabled)\nPer GKR round with precompute-M:\n- **`precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`**: `num_blocks = ⌈2^{tail_n} / tail_tile⌉` instances\n  - tail_n = rem_n - w (rem_n ≈ 18 for total_rounds=20, w=3)\n  - tail_tile ≈ 2048 → num_blocks ≈ 256 instances per window\n  - **Multiple windows per GKR round** (one window processes w=3 rounds)\n  - Total M-build kernels: 256 * (num_windows) ≈ 256 * 6 = **~1536 instances per GKR round**\n\n**Critical observation**: Precompute-M creates 10x more kernel instances than fold-eval, but gains locality by amortizing M computation over w rounds.\n\n---\n\n### **5. DATA FLOW & SYNCHRONIZATION**\n\n#### **Main Buffers**\n\n| Buffer | Size | Purpose | Lifetime |\n|--------|------|---------|----------|\n| `layer` | 2^n Frac<EF> | Tree & current pq evaluations | Entire computation |\n| `work_buffer` | Depends on strategy | Ping-pong for non-last GKR rounds | Per GKR round |\n| `d_sum_evals` | 2 EF (sometimes more) | GPU output of s'(1), s'(2) | Per sumcheck round |\n| `tmp_block_sums` | `num_blocks * 2` EF | Block-wise partial sums | Per compute kernel |\n| `eq_buffer` | Sqrt-decomposed | eq(xi_prev[j..], y) for tail y | Per GKR round |\n| `m_buffer` | 2^{2w} EF | Full precomputed M matrix | Per precompute-M window |\n| `m_partial_buffer` | `num_blocks * 2^{2w}` EF | Partial M blocks | Per precompute-M build |\n| `eq_r_prefix/suffix_buffer` | 2^w each | MLE tables for M-eval | Per precompute-M window |\n\n#### **Read/Write Patterns**\n\n**Tree Build Kernels**:\n- **Read**: `layer[idx]`, `layer[idx + half]` (coalesced within warp)\n- **Write**: `layer[idx]` (in-place overwrite)\n- **Sync**: Implicit kernel barrier\n\n**Compute+Fold Kernels**:\n- **Read**: \n  - `eq_xi_low[...]`, `eq_xi_high[...]` (weights)\n  - `pq[idx]`, `pq[idx + quarter]`, `pq[idx + eighth]`, `pq[idx + 3*eighth]` (current & folded values)\n- **Write**:\n  - `dst_pq[idx]`, `dst_pq[idx + quarter]`, ... (folded output)\n  - `block_sums[block * 2]` (partial sums)\n- **Synchronization**: \n  - Kernel barrier after compute\n  - Block sums fully reduced by separate `static_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel\n  - Block sums copied back to CPU for `s'(1), s'(2)` reconstruction\n\n**Precompute-M Flow**:\n1. `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`: Reads pq, writes `partial_M` (parallel over tail tiles)\n2. Implicit kernel barrier\n3. `precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel`: Reads `partial_M`, writes `m_total`\n4. Implicit kernel barrier\n5. Loop w times: `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel` (reads m_total, writes 2 EF)\n   - CPU calls `observe_and_update()`, samples challenge\n6. `multifold_kernel`: Reads pq, writes folded pq\n\n---\n\n### **6. INDEPENDENCE & PARALLELIZABILITY**\n\n#### **Within a Single Trace (Intra-Trace)**\n✅ **Fully independent, parallelizable**:\n- Each thread idx processes distinct memory addresses\n- No inter-thread communication except block reductions\n- Block reductions are standard parallel primitives (no data dependencies)\n\n**Examples**:\n- Tree build: thread 0 processes (layer[0], layer[half]), thread 1 processes (layer[1], layer[half+1]), etc.\n- Compute+fold: thread idx processes 4 pairs at specific offsets\n- Multifold: thread idx folds tail index idx independently\n\n#### **Per-Sumcheck Round (Intra-Round)**\n✅ **Mostly independent**:\n- GPU kernel launches are sequential (data dependency on fold from previous round)\n- Block reductions are data-parallel\n- CPU operations (observe_and_update, sample_ext) are sequential\n\n**Potential parallelization** (not in current code):\n- Could parallelize block reductions across different blocks (within one kernel)\n- Could overlap CPU processing with next GPU kernel launch (current code is synchronous)\n\n#### **Between GKR Rounds (Inter-Round)**\n❌ **Sequential by protocol**:\n- Each GKR round generates new challenges (mu, rho)\n- Next round depends on these challenges\n- No parallelism between rounds due to transcript dependencies\n\n---\n\n### **7. CONCRETE KERNEL LAUNCH PARAMETERS**\n\n#### **Example: compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel for num_x = 2^18**\n\n```\nnum_x = 2^18 = 262,144\nelements = num_x / 2 = 131,072\ndefault_block_size = 256\nblocks_needed = 131,072 / 256 = 512 blocks\n\nAdaptive sizing:\n  min_blocks_target_for_device = 228 (or based on SM count)\n  blocks_needed (512) >= min_blocks_target (228) ✓\n  → Use default_block_size = 256\n\nLaunch:\n  Grid: 512 blocks\n  Block: 256 threads\n  Shared mem: ⌈256 / 32⌉ * sizeof(FpExt) = 8 * 32 bytes = 256 bytes\n  Total threads: 512 * 256 = 131,072 (one per element)\n```\n\n#### **Example: precompute_m_build_partial_kernel with rem_n=18, w=3, tail_tile=2048**\n\n```\ntail_n = 18 - 3 = 15\nk = 2^15 = 32,768\nnum_blocks = ⌈32,768 / 2,048⌉ = 16 blocks\n\nEach block:\n  Grid: 16 blocks\n  Block: (8, 8) = 64 threads (one per M entry)\n  Shared mem: 64 bytes * (4 * (8+1) * 16 + 16) ≈ ~22.5 KB per block\n  \nPer-block parallelism:\n  64 threads process tail_tile=2,048 tail points\n  64 threads / 2,048 tail points → stride loop: each thread processes 32 tail points\n```\n\n---\n\n### **8. BUFFER USAGE & MEMORY TRAFFIC**\n\n#### **Peak Memory Usage**\n- `layer`: 2^n * 16 bytes (two EF per Frac)\n- `work_buffer`: 2^(n-2) * 16 bytes (for non-last GKR rounds)\n- `tmp_block_sums`: num_blocks * 2 * 32 bytes (small, ~100 KB)\n- `eq_buffer`: O(n²) total (sqrt-decomposed, reused)\n- `m_buffer`: 2^(2w) * 32 bytes = 4,096 * 32 = 128 KB (w=3)\n\n#### **Global Memory Traffic (Per Sumcheck Round)**\n- **Tree build layers**: Read 2 elements, write 1 element per thread → ~3 loads/store per element, ~3n total traffic\n- **Compute+fold**: Read 4 pq pairs + 2 eq weights, write 1 pq pair + 1 block sum → ~5 reads, 2 writes per element\n  - For full hypercube: ~5 * 2^n * 32 + 2 * 2^n * 32 = 7 * 2^n * 32 bytes\n- **Multifold**: Read 2^w coefficients from src, write 2 outputs → ~2^w reads + 2 writes per output element\n\n---\n\n### **Key Observations & Design Insights**\n\n1. **Kernel fusion is critical**: Fusing fold + compute eliminates one kernel launch and reduces GMem traffic by ~30% (fold + compute together vs separately)\n\n2. **Per-trace independence**: All GPU operations are embarrassingly parallel within a trace (thread-level parallelism), enabling scaling to massive thread counts\n\n3. **Sequential between rounds**: The algorithm is inherently sequential at the GKR round level due to transcript dependencies (sampling challenges)\n\n4. **Precompute-M trades latency for locality**: By precomputing a small M matrix, it amortizes the cost of 3 rounds into one M-build, reducing overall memory throughput requirements at the cost of extra kernel launches and temporary storage\n\n5. **Adaptive grid sizing**: Dynamically reduces block size for small workloads to ensure adequate occupancy and SM coverage\n\n6. **In-place tree**: Clever use of reversible fractional addition allows maintaining a single buffer instead of a full tree materialization, saving 2x memory"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8752a685-980c-4a43-bd75-41a76d7996d5","uuid":"3ce930cc-95b2-4be0-a5a0-159d57cbd429","timestamp":"2026-04-13T18:38:52.170Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the GPU implementation of the fractional sumcheck GKR tree computation in the stark-backend CUDA prover. This is the code path that runs after GKR input evaluation and computes the GKR protocol tree.\n\nSpecifically, I need to understand:\n1. Where `fractional_sumcheck_gkr` kernels are launched from Rust code\n2. The structure of the tree computation: how many levels, what happens at each level, what the per-trace vs per-level operations are\n3. The CUDA kernels: `compute_round_and_revert_kernel`, `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel` (1370 instances!), `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`, `multifold_kernel`, `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`, `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`\n4. Whether per-trace operations within a level/round are independent (parallelizable)\n5. The data flow: what buffers are read/written, where are the synchronization points\n\nStart with:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/` — look for files related to GKR, fractional_sumcheck\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/` — look for fractional_sumcheck_gkr CUDA kernels\n\nReport the full call chain from the Rust prover entry point through to the GPU kernel launches, with file paths and line numbers. Focus on identifying which operations are per-trace (independent) vs per-level (sequential), and where synchronization happens.\n\nThoroughness level: very thorough","agentId":"a0ad498c8a42b468e","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have comprehensive information. Let me create a detailed summary:\n\n## Summary: GPU Implementation of Fractional Sumcheck GKR Tree Computation\n\n### **High-Level Overview**\n\nThe GPU implementation of fractional sumcheck GKR computation is a complex system that:\n1. **Builds a segment tree** from leaf fractions via in-place frac_add operations\n2. **Runs GKR rounds** iteratively, each with a multi-round sumcheck\n3. **Uses adaptive strategies**: either \"fold-eval\" (one variable per round) or \"precompute-M\" (w variables at once)\n4. **Fuses operations** aggressively to reduce kernel launches and memory traffic\n\n---\n\n### **1. CALL CHAIN FROM RUST ENTRY POINT TO GPU**\n\n#### **Entry Point**\n**File**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`  \n**Function**: `fractional_sumcheck_gpu()` (lines 491–1091)\n\nThis is the main GPU prover that orchestrates the entire GKR tree computation.\n\n#### **Call Chain: Segment Tree Build Phase**\n\n```\nfractional_sumcheck_gpu()\n  ├─ [Large leaves] bit_rev_frac_ext_build_k2() ──→ CUDA: _bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel (line 1339)\n  │   Purpose: Fused bitrev + tree layers 0+1 for domain_size > 1024 (line 529)\n  │\n  ├─ [Small leaves] bit_rev_frac_ext() ──→ CUDA: bit_rev_frac_ext() (separate kernel)\n  │   Purpose: Bitrev only for domain_size ≤ 1024 (line 537)\n  │\n  ├─ frac_build_tree_layer() ──→ CUDA: _frac_build_tree_layer_kernel (line 116)\n  │   Purpose: Single tree layer build (line 545)\n  │\n  ├─ Loop: frac_build_tree_two_layers() ──→ CUDA: _frac_build_tree_two_layers (line 856)\n  │   Purpose: Fused two-layer kernel, ~33% traffic savings (lines 561–567)\n  │   Instances: ⌈(total_rounds - start_layer_i) / 2⌉ times\n  │\n  ├─ frac_build_tree_layer() (revert=true) ──→ CUDA: _frac_build_tree_layer_kernel<true> (line 581)\n  │   Purpose: Revert root layer to recover first layer's two children (line 581)\n  │\n  └─ [All tree operations complete; tree maintained in-place in single buffer]\n```\n\n**Kernel Launch Details (Segment Tree)**:\n- **`bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`** (line 1339):\n  - Threads: 256/block, Grid: `(domain_size / (256 * 8))`\n  - Shared mem: 64 KB/block\n  - Fuses: bitrev + tree layers 0+1 in one kernel\n  \n- **`frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`** (line 28):\n  - Threads: 256/block (default from `kernel_launch_params`)\n  - Grid: `⌈half / 256⌉` blocks\n  - Per-layer operation: parallelizes over half-sized output elements\n  \n- **`frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`** (line 856):\n  - Threads: 256/block (reduced from 1024 for register headroom, line 918)\n  - Grid: `⌈half_i1 / 256⌉` blocks\n  - Fuses two consecutive layers\n\n---\n\n#### **Call Chain: GKR Rounds Phase**\n\n**Main Loop**: `for round in 1..total_rounds` (line 655)\n\nFor each GKR round `j = round + 1`:\n\n```\nGKR Round j (code round):\n  ├─ Setup eq_buffer: SqrtEqLayers::from_xi(&xi_prev[1..]) (line 663)\n  │\n  ├─ First sumcheck round (round 0): ALWAYS via do_sumcheck_round_and_revert()\n  │   ├─ frac_compute_round_and_revert() ──→ CUDA: _frac_compute_round_and_revert (line 144)\n  │   │   Calls: compute_round_and_revert_kernel (line 1037) + final_reduce_block_sums\n  │   │   Fuses: tree layer revert + sumcheck compute round 0\n  │   │\n  │   └─ eq_buffer.drop_layer() (line 381) — no GPU kernel, CPU side\n  │\n  ├─ Remaining sumcheck rounds (rounds 1 to round-1): Choose strategy (line 680)\n  │\n  └─ Strategy 1: FoldEval (line 718–819)\n     ├─ For each remaining xi_j:\n     │   ├─ BufferScheduler decides target (ping-pong vs in-place, lines 720–787)\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ do_fused_sumcheck_round(src_buffer, dst_buffer) ──→ CUDA: _frac_compute_round_and_fold\n     │   │   Calls: compute_round_and_fold_kernel (line 235) + final_reduce_block_sums\n     │   │   Fuses: fold by r_prev + compute s'(1), s'(2)\n     │   │   **Out-of-place** (ping-pong between layer & work_buffer)\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ do_fused_sumcheck_round_inplace(buffer) ──→ CUDA: _frac_compute_round_and_fold_inplace\n     │   │   Calls: compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel (line 323) + final_reduce_block_sums\n     │   │   **In-place** (reads 2*pq_size, writes pq_size to same buffer)\n     │   │\n     │   └─ eq_buffer.drop_layer() (line 431, 477)\n     │\n     └─ Final fold: fold_ef_frac_columns() ──→ CUDA: _frac_fold_fpext_columns (line 155)\n        Calls: fold_ef_columns_kernel (line 204)\n        Standalone kernel, no compute (final inner round)\n\n  └─ Strategy 2: PrecomputeM (line 821–1054)\n     ├─ Loop: while base < stop (line 853)\n     │   ├─ Choose window width w (line 856)\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ Build M-matrix: frac_precompute_m_build_raw() ──→ CUDA: _frac_precompute_m_build (line 193)\n     │   │   Calls: precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold, W> (line 515)\n     │   │           + precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel (line 1211)\n     │   │   Instances: ONE per tail_tile (num_blocks computed at line 893)\n     │   │   Purpose: Compute M matrix with tail-parallelism\n     │   │\n     │   ├─ For each round t in window (w rounds):\n     │   │   ├─ frac_precompute_m_eval_round_raw() ──→ CUDA: _frac_precompute_m_eval_round (line 209)\n     │   │   │   Calls: precompute_m_eval_round_kernel (line 1233)\n     │   │   │   Grid: 1 block (small kernel operating on 2^{2w} M-matrix)\n     │   │   │   Purpose: Extract s'_t(1), s'_t(2) from M\n     │   │   │\n     │   │   ├─ observe_and_update() (CPU, line 955)\n     │   │   │   Reconstructs s evaluations from GPU output\n     │   │   │   Samples challenge r\n     │   │   │\n     │   │   └─ eq_buffer.drop_layer() (line 954)\n     │   │\n     │   └─ Multifold: frac_multifold_raw() ──→ CUDA: _frac_multifold (line 218)\n     │       Calls: multifold_kernel<W> (line 783, templated on W)\n     │       Grid: `⌈tail_size / 256⌉`\n     │       Purpose: Fold w (or w+1) variables at once\n     │\n     ├─ Tail rounds (if any): Fallback to fold-eval path (lines 1002–1045)\n     │\n     └─ Final fold: fold_ef_frac_columns_inplace() (line 1049)\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. TREE STRUCTURE: LEVELS, PER-TRACE VS PER-LEVEL OPERATIONS**\n\n#### **Segment Tree Layout**\n- **Total Rounds**: `total_rounds = log2(total_leaves)` = n\n- **Layers**: n+1 layers (layer 0 = root, layer n = leaves)\n- **Memory**: Single buffer of size `2^n` (input leaves), reused in-place\n- **Ordering**: Bit-reversed for coalesced memory access\n\n#### **Per-Trace Operations (INDEPENDENT, PARALLELIZABLE)**\nEach tree layer build is a per-trace operation:\n- **Thread i processes**: pairs `(layer[i], layer[i + half])`\n- **No data dependencies between threads** within a layer\n- **All threads can run in parallel** (different indices → different memory addresses)\n- **Synchronization**: Only implicit via kernel barrier at end\n\n**Examples**:\n1. `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`: Each thread `idx` computes `layer[idx] = frac_add(layer[idx], layer[idx + half])` independently\n2. `multifold_kernel`: Each thread processes a unique tail index independently\n3. `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`: 2D block grid where each thread (u,v) accumulates M[u,v] over independent tail tiles\n\n#### **Per-Level Operations (SEQUENTIAL)**\n- **Levels must be processed sequentially**: output of level i becomes input to level i+1\n- **Level i must complete** before level i+1 starts (implicit via kernel launch sequencing)\n- **No loop over tree levels in GPU code**: tree building is fused into launchers\n\n#### **Synchronization Points**\n1. **After each kernel launch**: implicit host-side sync via kernel completion\n2. **eq_buffer.drop_layer()**: CPU-side operation (no GPU sync needed)\n3. **Tree reverts**: Sequential per round (one layer per GKR round)\n4. **Block reductions**: Implicit via `static_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel\n\n---\n\n### **3. KEY CUDA KERNELS: STRUCTURE & BEHAVIOR**\n\n#### **A. Segment Tree Kernels**\n\n| Kernel | File | Line | Purpose | Fused With |\n|--------|------|------|---------|-----------|\n| `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel` | gkr.cu | 1339 | Bitrev + tree layers 0+1 | Bitrev + 2-layer tree |\n| `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel` | gkr.cu | 28 | Single layer tree build/revert | Nothing |\n| `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel` | gkr.cu | 856 | Two consecutive tree layers | 2-layer build |\n\n**Thread Model**:\n- Each thread idx: `layer[idx] = op(layer[idx], layer[idx + half])`\n- **Independence**: Each thread's read/write addresses are unique → no conflicts\n- **Parallelism**: Linear in `half` (output size)\n\n---\n\n#### **B. Sumcheck Compute Kernels**\n\n| Kernel | Fused Ops | In-Place? | Outputs |\n|--------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n| `compute_round_and_revert_kernel` | Revert + Compute | YES | s'(1), s'(2) |\n| `compute_round_and_fold_kernel` | Compute + Fold | NO | s'(1), s'(2) + folded pq |\n| `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel` | Compute + Fold | YES | s'(1), s'(2) + folded pq |\n| `compute_round_block_sum_kernel` | Compute only | N/A | s'(1), s'(2) |\n\n**All 4 kernels**:\n- **Per-trace parallelism**: YES (each thread processes independent pairs)\n- **Thread idx**: processes pair `idx` over all 2^n hypercube points\n- **Local accumulators**: local0 (for s'(1)), local1 (for s'(2))\n- **Block reduction**: Sum-reduce within block, write to `tmp_block_sums[block * 2 + i]`\n- **Final reduction**: Separate kernel reduces block sums → final output (2 values)\n\n**Critical fusion**: Fold + Compute are fused to reduce kernel launches and GMem traffic:\n- Fuses 2 kernels (fold + compute) → 1 kernel + 1 reduction kernel\n- Each trace: folds 4 pairs of pq values simultaneously with compute\n\n---\n\n#### **C. Precompute-M Kernels**\n\n| Kernel | Line | Purpose | Parallelism |\n|--------|------|---------|-------------|\n| `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold, W>` | 515 | Build M-matrix partial blocks | Over tail points |\n| `precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel` | 1211 | Reduce partial M blocks | Over M entries |\n| `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel` | 1233 | Extract s'(t) from M | 1 block only |\n| `multifold_kernel<W>` | 783 | Fold w variables at once | Over tail indices |\n\n**M-build (line 515)**:\n- **Grid**: `num_blocks = ⌈2^{tail_n} / tail_tile⌉` (1 block per tail_tile points)\n- **Block**: `(2^w, 2^w)` threads = m² threads, one per M entry (u,v)\n- **Operation per thread**: Accumulate contribution `M[u,v] += sum over tail_tile`\n- **Per-tail-block independence**: Each block processes disjoint tail points → parallelizable\n- **Shared memory**: `(4 * (2^w + 1) * tail_batch + tail_batch) * sizeof(FpExt)` with bank-conflict padding\n\n**M-reduce (line 1211)**:\n- **Grid**: `⌈2^{2w} / 128⌉` blocks\n- **Operation**: Sum over all `num_blocks` partial contributions for each M entry\n- **Block reduction**: Each block sums 128 entries in parallel\n\n**M-eval (line 1233)**:\n- **Grid**: 1 block, 256 threads\n- **Operation**: Contract M with eq_r_prefix and eq_suffix to compute s'(1), s'(2)\n- **Small kernel**: operates on 2^{2w} M-matrix (w=3 → 64x64 = 4096 entries)\n\n**Multifold (line 783)**:\n- **Grid**: `⌈tail_size / 256⌉` blocks\n- **Block**: 256 threads\n- **Thread out_idx**: computes output `[out_idx, tail_size + out_idx]` (two poly outputs)\n- **Operation**: For each of 2^w coefficients, sum-accumulate weighted by eq_r_window\n\n---\n\n### **4. INSTANCE COUNTS FOR KEY KERNELS**\n\n#### **Segment Tree Build**\n- **`bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`**: 1 instance (if domain_size > 1024)\n- **`frac_build_tree_layer_kernel`**: 1 instance (first layer after bitrev, or fallback)\n- **`frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`**: `⌈(total_rounds - start_layer_i) / 2⌉` instances\n  - Typical: 10 instances for 20-round computation\n\n#### **GKR Rounds (Total Sumcheck Rounds)**\n- **Total sumcheck rounds across all GKR rounds**: `∑(round) = 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (total_rounds-1) = O(total_rounds²)`\n  - For total_rounds=20: 0+1+2+...+19 = 190 sumcheck rounds\n\n#### **Per-GKR-Round Kernel Instances** (assuming fold-eval strategy)\nFor GKR round j (code: round):\n- **`compute_round_and_revert_kernel`**: 1 instance (first sumcheck round 0)\n- **`compute_round_and_fold_kernel`** or **`compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel`**: `round - 1` instances (sumcheck rounds 1 to round-1)\n- **`fold_ef_columns_kernel`**: 1 instance (final fold after last sumcheck round)\n- **`static_final_reduce_block_sums`**: 1 instance per compute kernel (reduces block sums)\n\n**Total for all GKR rounds** (fold-eval path, total_rounds=20):\n- `compute_round_and_revert_kernel`: 19 instances (one per GKR round 1..19)\n- Fused compute+fold kernels: 0+1+2+...+18 = 171 instances\n- Fold-only kernels: 19 instances\n- **~210 kernel launches total** (dominated by fused compute+fold)\n\n#### **Precompute-M Path** (if enabled)\nPer GKR round with precompute-M:\n- **`precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`**: `num_blocks = ⌈2^{tail_n} / tail_tile⌉` instances\n  - tail_n = rem_n - w (rem_n ≈ 18 for total_rounds=20, w=3)\n  - tail_tile ≈ 2048 → num_blocks ≈ 256 instances per window\n  - **Multiple windows per GKR round** (one window processes w=3 rounds)\n  - Total M-build kernels: 256 * (num_windows) ≈ 256 * 6 = **~1536 instances per GKR round**\n\n**Critical observation**: Precompute-M creates 10x more kernel instances than fold-eval, but gains locality by amortizing M computation over w rounds.\n\n---\n\n### **5. DATA FLOW & SYNCHRONIZATION**\n\n#### **Main Buffers**\n\n| Buffer | Size | Purpose | Lifetime |\n|--------|------|---------|----------|\n| `layer` | 2^n Frac<EF> | Tree & current pq evaluations | Entire computation |\n| `work_buffer` | Depends on strategy | Ping-pong for non-last GKR rounds | Per GKR round |\n| `d_sum_evals` | 2 EF (sometimes more) | GPU output of s'(1), s'(2) | Per sumcheck round |\n| `tmp_block_sums` | `num_blocks * 2` EF | Block-wise partial sums | Per compute kernel |\n| `eq_buffer` | Sqrt-decomposed | eq(xi_prev[j..], y) for tail y | Per GKR round |\n| `m_buffer` | 2^{2w} EF | Full precomputed M matrix | Per precompute-M window |\n| `m_partial_buffer` | `num_blocks * 2^{2w}` EF | Partial M blocks | Per precompute-M build |\n| `eq_r_prefix/suffix_buffer` | 2^w each | MLE tables for M-eval | Per precompute-M window |\n\n#### **Read/Write Patterns**\n\n**Tree Build Kernels**:\n- **Read**: `layer[idx]`, `layer[idx + half]` (coalesced within warp)\n- **Write**: `layer[idx]` (in-place overwrite)\n- **Sync**: Implicit kernel barrier\n\n**Compute+Fold Kernels**:\n- **Read**: \n  - `eq_xi_low[...]`, `eq_xi_high[...]` (weights)\n  - `pq[idx]`, `pq[idx + quarter]`, `pq[idx + eighth]`, `pq[idx + 3*eighth]` (current & folded values)\n- **Write**:\n  - `dst_pq[idx]`, `dst_pq[idx + quarter]`, ... (folded output)\n  - `block_sums[block * 2]` (partial sums)\n- **Synchronization**: \n  - Kernel barrier after compute\n  - Block sums fully reduced by separate `static_final_reduce_block_sums` kernel\n  - Block sums copied back to CPU for `s'(1), s'(2)` reconstruction\n\n**Precompute-M Flow**:\n1. `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel`: Reads pq, writes `partial_M` (parallel over tail tiles)\n2. Implicit kernel barrier\n3. `precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel`: Reads `partial_M`, writes `m_total`\n4. Implicit kernel barrier\n5. Loop w times: `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel` (reads m_total, writes 2 EF)\n   - CPU calls `observe_and_update()`, samples challenge\n6. `multifold_kernel`: Reads pq, writes folded pq\n\n---\n\n### **6. INDEPENDENCE & PARALLELIZABILITY**\n\n#### **Within a Single Trace (Intra-Trace)**\n✅ **Fully independent, parallelizable**:\n- Each thread idx processes distinct memory addresses\n- No inter-thread communication except block reductions\n- Block reductions are standard parallel primitives (no data dependencies)\n\n**Examples**:\n- Tree build: thread 0 processes (layer[0], layer[half]), thread 1 processes (layer[1], layer[half+1]), etc.\n- Compute+fold: thread idx processes 4 pairs at specific offsets\n- Multifold: thread idx folds tail index idx independently\n\n#### **Per-Sumcheck Round (Intra-Round)**\n✅ **Mostly independent**:\n- GPU kernel launches are sequential (data dependency on fold from previous round)\n- Block reductions are data-parallel\n- CPU operations (observe_and_update, sample_ext) are sequential\n\n**Potential parallelization** (not in current code):\n- Could parallelize block reductions across different blocks (within one kernel)\n- Could overlap CPU processing with next GPU kernel launch (current code is synchronous)\n\n#### **Between GKR Rounds (Inter-Round)**\n❌ **Sequential by protocol**:\n- Each GKR round generates new challenges (mu, rho)\n- Next round depends on these challenges\n- No parallelism between rounds due to transcript dependencies\n\n---\n\n### **7. CONCRETE KERNEL LAUNCH PARAMETERS**\n\n#### **Example: compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel for num_x = 2^18**\n\n```\nnum_x = 2^18 = 262,144\nelements = num_x / 2 = 131,072\ndefault_block_size = 256\nblocks_needed = 131,072 / 256 = 512 blocks\n\nAdaptive sizing:\n  min_blocks_target_for_device = 228 (or based on SM count)\n  blocks_needed (512) >= min_blocks_target (228) ✓\n  → Use default_block_size = 256\n\nLaunch:\n  Grid: 512 blocks\n  Block: 256 threads\n  Shared mem: ⌈256 / 32⌉ * sizeof(FpExt) = 8 * 32 bytes = 256 bytes\n  Total threads: 512 * 256 = 131,072 (one per element)\n```\n\n#### **Example: precompute_m_build_partial_kernel with rem_n=18, w=3, tail_tile=2048**\n\n```\ntail_n = 18 - 3 = 15\nk = 2^15 = 32,768\nnum_blocks = ⌈32,768 / 2,048⌉ = 16 blocks\n\nEach block:\n  Grid: 16 blocks\n  Block: (8, 8) = 64 threads (one per M entry)\n  Shared mem: 64 bytes * (4 * (8+1) * 16 + 16) ≈ ~22.5 KB per block\n  \nPer-block parallelism:\n  64 threads process tail_tile=2,048 tail points\n  64 threads / 2,048 tail points → stride loop: each thread processes 32 tail points\n```\n\n---\n\n### **8. BUFFER USAGE & MEMORY TRAFFIC**\n\n#### **Peak Memory Usage**\n- `layer`: 2^n * 16 bytes (two EF per Frac)\n- `work_buffer`: 2^(n-2) * 16 bytes (for non-last GKR rounds)\n- `tmp_block_sums`: num_blocks * 2 * 32 bytes (small, ~100 KB)\n- `eq_buffer`: O(n²) total (sqrt-decomposed, reused)\n- `m_buffer`: 2^(2w) * 32 bytes = 4,096 * 32 = 128 KB (w=3)\n\n#### **Global Memory Traffic (Per Sumcheck Round)**\n- **Tree build layers**: Read 2 elements, write 1 element per thread → ~3 loads/store per element, ~3n total traffic\n- **Compute+fold**: Read 4 pq pairs + 2 eq weights, write 1 pq pair + 1 block sum → ~5 reads, 2 writes per element\n  - For full hypercube: ~5 * 2^n * 32 + 2 * 2^n * 32 = 7 * 2^n * 32 bytes\n- **Multifold**: Read 2^w coefficients from src, write 2 outputs → ~2^w reads + 2 writes per output element\n\n---\n\n### **Key Observations & Design Insights**\n\n1. **Kernel fusion is critical**: Fusing fold + compute eliminates one kernel launch and reduces GMem traffic by ~30% (fold + compute together vs separately)\n\n2. **Per-trace independence**: All GPU operations are embarrassingly parallel within a trace (thread-level parallelism), enabling scaling to massive thread counts\n\n3. **Sequential between rounds**: The algorithm is inherently sequential at the GKR round level due to transcript dependencies (sampling challenges)\n\n4. **Precompute-M trades latency for locality**: By precomputing a small M matrix, it amortizes the cost of 3 rounds into one M-build, reducing overall memory throughput requirements at the cost of extra kernel launches and temporary storage\n\n5. **Adaptive grid sizing**: Dynamically reduces block size for small workloads to ensure adequate occupancy and SM coverage\n\n6. **In-place tree**: Clever use of reversible fractional addition allows maintaining a single buffer instead of a full tree materialization, saving 2x memory"}],"totalDurationMs":112594,"totalTokens":79906,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8459,"cache_read_input_tokens":64533,"output_tokens":6911,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8459},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":6911,"cache_read_input_tokens":64533,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8459,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8459,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount