eakdown, GKR Input Eval dominates at 318ms across both segments, while the fractional sumcheck (GKR tree) takes another 214ms. The round-robin would likely save 11-19ms from input eval, but the tree component hasn't been optimized at all—even a modest 10% improvement there could yield 21ms. The tree is trickier since it's a sequential protocol, though there might be launch overhead worth reducing. I have detailed research from the subagent on this already.\n\nThe `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel` runs 1370 times averaging 6.5μs each, but with ~10μs launch overhead per call, that's 14ms of overhead against only 9ms of actual computation. The challenge is that each round depends on the previous one's challenge from the transcript, so they can't be batched. Fusing consecutive fold and compute operations into a single kernel would require algorithmic changes since the challenge must be sampled between rounds.\n\nI'm settling on GKR round-robin as the optimization target—it's the best risk-adjusted choice. But let me double-check the numbers. The GKR input eval has 8 threads with pre-allocated buffers and potentially larger, more GPU-saturating kernels. Round 0 round-robin saved 22ms with a 26ms spread across segments, so GKR might have different characteristics. At APC 300, the GKR input eval processes ~310 AIRs per segment with widely varying heights. Looking at the prealloc report, there's a dramatic asymmetry: seg0 takes 256ms while seg1 only takes 62ms.\n\nFor seg1, contiguous chunking would give each of the 8 threads roughly 39 AIRs, with thread 0 getting the largest ones and thread 7 the smallest. Based on Round 0's pattern where round-robin improved the spread from 26ms to 7ms, GKR could see a similar ~19ms improvement—though that's substantial relative to seg1's 62ms total, suggesting the actual thread spread is probably 10-15ms. For seg0's 256ms, the spread could be 20-50ms depending on how much imbalance exists in the kernel execution for larger AIRs.\n\